    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add Art 29-D Title 1 §2997-f-1, Pub Health L",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S2248\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the public health law, in relation\nto electronic health records\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nTo ensure certain health care facilities utilize electronic health\nrecords and connect to regional health information organizations in\norder to facilitate the exchange of health information.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection one adds a new Public Health Law (PHL) § 2997-f-1 to require\nall Article 28 hospitals, office-based surgery practices under PHL §\n230-d, and Urgent Care or similar providers that offer extended hours\nand accept unscheduled, walk-in appointments from patients that are\nnot regularly seen by the practitioner to utilize and maintain an\nelectronic health record (EHR) system that connects to the local\nregional health information organization (RHIO) in order to facilitate\nthe exchange of health information. Each RHIO must ensure it is\naccessible and capable of connecting all health care providers or\nqualified entities and their EHR vendor. Providers may apply for any\navailable funding for health information technology and electronic\nhealth record infrastructure to achieve the requirements of this\nsection. This section also authorizes the Commissioner to implement a\nwaiver process to exempt providers from this section due to economic\nhardship or technological limitations outside of the control of the\nprovider, or other exceptional circumstances.\n\nSection two provides that this act take effect in two years; however,\neffective immediately, the Commissioner is authorized to promulgate\nregulations necessary to implement.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nNew York's health care infrastructure is undergoing a great deal of\ntransformation. New York is home to emerging models of care that\nconsumers are opting to utilize, such as \"minute clinics\" and urgent\ncare facilities. Consumers may opt to have many procedures, which\nthanks to medical advances, can safely be performed in ambulatory and\noffice-based settings, rather than visiting a hospital. The Delivery\nSystem Reform Incentive Payment (DSRIP) Program pursuant to the\nfederal waiver with the Centers for Medicare and Medicaid (CMS)\ndemands a decrease in hospital admissions in exchange for a focus on\nproviding community based care. While these consumer driven, community\nmodels of care thrive, health care reform continues its focus on\nprimary, preventative medicine and managing the care of entire\npatient. This legislation ensures that information regarding a\npatient's visit to an urgent care or similar facility providing\nepisodic care will make it into the patient's health care record so\nthat it can be accessed by the patient, the patient's primary care\nprovider, and other connected facilities the patient may subsequently\nutilize. Connecting these providers will also ensure they have access\nto the patient's existing health record, enabling them to provide the\npatient with the best care.\n\nAs part of this system transformation, the state has spent billions of\ndollars building an infrastructure, the Statewide Health Information\nNetwork of New York (SHIN-NY) and bringing all regional health\ninformation organizations (RHI0s) onto a level playing field, in order\nto facilitate this sharing of health information. CMS, pursuant to the\nfederal waiver, requires that certain providers connect and share\ninformation. By bringing together more providers, this legislation\nwill help advance the success of this network and achieve the trip aim\nof improving health, enhancing quality and reducing cost.\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\n2015-2016: S.5094-A - Passed Senate\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nTwo years; provided the Commissioner is authorized to promulgate\nregulations immediately.\n",
